the end of the federal era is a huge  blow to tennis and his millions of fans  worldwide but in true fedra-esque style  he's found the perfect way to bow out  reuniting the big four against the rest  of the world on the same side of the net  as his greatest rival and friend Rafa  Nadal and in London the city of many of  his iconic moments I just felt it would  be wonderful having Borg on the chair  having the greatest players on the  planet right now alongside me and I just  felt it wouldn't feel lonely you know  because often when you retire you're  alone in the court you just probably  lost your last match and you're all  emotional talking how much it meant to  you being in the game and I thought I  didn't want that I didn't want that sad  moment I wanted to be happy and more  like a party party mode I was able to to  to have an amazing to create an amazing  rivalry together  and in the hand something that probably  we are very proud I have been a friendly  driver you know it's 20 grand  including the eight Wimbledon titles  have all been achieved with the style of  a ballet dancer his movement and  effortless power inspiring many players  but replicated by none of them  relatively untroubled by injury for a  quarter of a century the injured knee  that's kept him out of tennis since  Wimbledon 2021 has finally said enough  is enough I think it should be good  enough for a doubles to be honest but  for the singles uh honestly I haven't  trained at all the last three months and  it would just be too much to ask for for  the the body and I am not willing to put  all the risk on the line for that Roger  Federer and his agent Tony godzick came  with the Laver cup to honor his Idol Rod  Laver and in a way to mimic the Ryder  Cup he long ago eclipsed his hero in  terms of titles and when he finally says  goodbye to tennis it is going to be a  special moment in sporting history  Federer has given so much to the sport  it's the end of an era and there will be  tears on and off the court  Jackie Bell trout Sky News
 

